Subject: [PATCH] holographic store: roll back the failed-INSERT transaction

On the duplicate-content path the store returned an existing id WITHOUT ending
the transaction. The failed INSERT had already opened a deferred write txn, so
returning early stranded it open on a long-lived connection -- locking every
other writer out of memory_store.db (memory_ingest's provenance and overlay
writes, the sync crons) until the process exited.

This is a BUG FIX, not a preference. An image built from pristine upstream
reintroduces a database-wide write lock that surfaces as intermittent
"database is locked" errors under concurrent memory writes.

Discovered 2026-07-25 as an uncommitted modification in the running checkout;
the Phase 0 audit recorded only one local patch and missed this one. See
docs/00-CURRENT-STATE.md §12 and docs/OPERATIONS.md.

Verified to apply cleanly against a fresh clone of v2026.7.1.

diff --git a/plugins/memory/holographic/store.py b/plugins/memory/holographic/store.py
index 7ffc7db0f1..5f0e8765b3 100644
--- a/plugins/memory/holographic/store.py
+++ b/plugins/memory/holographic/store.py
@@ -171,7 +171,13 @@ class MemoryStore:
                 self._conn.commit()
                 fact_id: int = cur.lastrowid  # type: ignore[assignment]
             except sqlite3.IntegrityError:
-                # Duplicate content — return existing id
+                # Duplicate content — return existing id.
+                # Roll back first: the failed INSERT already opened a deferred
+                # write txn, and returning without commit/rollback strands it
+                # open on this long-lived connection, locking out every other
+                # connection to memory_store.db (memory_ingest's provenance and
+                # overlay writes, the sync crons) until the process exits.
+                self._conn.rollback()
                 row = self._conn.execute(
                     "SELECT fact_id FROM facts WHERE content = ?", (content,)
                 ).fetchone()
